  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can PDF metadata contradict what the document says?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. PDF metadata and visible document content are completely independent. A document can display a date of January 1st while its CreationDate metadata shows March 15th. It can say 'Final Version' while its RevisionNumber shows 47 saves. It can claim to be an original while its Producer field reveals it was processed by a PDF editor weeks after the stated date. The metadata is written automatically by software and reflects actual system behaviour, not what the author chose to display."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is a PDF metadata mismatch?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A PDF metadata mismatch occurs when the information stored in the PDF's hidden metadata fields, including CreationDate, ModDate, Author, Producer, and RevisionNumber, contradicts information visible in the document itself, such as the stated date, author name, version label, or claimed origin. These mismatches can be accidental (timezone conversion errors, template inheritance) or deliberate (backdating, concealing editing history)."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I check if a PDF's metadata matches its stated date?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Run exiftool document.pdf in Terminal or Command Prompt. Look at the Create Date and Modify Date fields. Compare these to the date printed on the document. If the Create Date is significantly later than the document's stated date, the document was created after that date. It was either backdated or based on a later template. FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover also shows these fields in its Privacy Risk Score view."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can you tell if a PDF was created from a template by looking at metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Often yes. If a PDF's CreationDate is recent but its Author field contains a name from years ago, or its RevisionNumber is very high for a supposedly new document, these are indicators of template reuse. More directly, the xmpMM:DocumentID field stays consistent across documents derived from the same template. Two 'independent' proposals with the same DocumentID came from the same source file."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Are PDF metadata mismatches illegal?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Accidental mismatches are not illegal. Deliberate mismatches created to deceive, including backdating a document to make it appear it was created before a legal deadline, falsifying authorship in court filings, or altering timestamps to support a fraudulent claim, can constitute fraud, evidence tampering, or forgery depending on jurisdiction. In the UK, submitting a deliberately backdated document as evidence may violate the Fraud Act 2006. In the US, it may violate 18 U.S.C. § 1519 (destruction, alteration, or falsification of records)."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I remove metadata mismatches from a PDF before sharing?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Use FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover. It strips all metadata fields including CreationDate, ModDate, Author, RevisionNumber, and the XMP stream in under 5 seconds, entirely in your browser. This removes the mismatch by eliminating the metadata entirely. For command line: exiftool -all= document.pdf"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

