    <div class="callout green">
      <div class="callout-label">&#10003; The cleanest approach</div>
      <p>Strip the metadata entirely before sharing rather than trying to manage individual field contradictions. A PDF with no metadata tells no story in either direction. Our complete guide on <a href="/blog/remove-metadata-from-pdf">how to remove metadata from a PDF</a> covers every method: browser, command line, and Acrobat. It also explains how to verify that both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream were fully cleared, not just partially.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="what-it-means-legally">#10. What metadata contradictions mean legally</h2>

    <p>The legal implications of metadata contradictions depend on whether the mismatch was accidental or deliberate, and whether it materially affected any party's decisions or rights.</p>

    <div class="legal-ref">
      <div class="lr-regime">US Federal Law</div>
      <div class="lr-title">18 U.S.C. § 1519: Destruction, alteration, or falsification of records</div>
      <div class="lr-text">Whoever knowingly alters, destroys, mutilates, conceals, covers up, falsifies, or makes a false entry in any record, document, or tangible object with the intent to impede, obstruct, or influence the investigation or proper administration of any matter within the jurisdiction of any department or agency of the United States... shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than 20 years, or both.</div>
      <div class="lr-source">Source: Cornell Law School: Legal Information Institute</div>
    </div>

    <p>Document backdating in the context of legal proceedings (creating a document after an event and timestamping it as if it predated the event) has been the basis for criminal charges in multiple US and UK cases. The metadata doesn't cause the legal problem. The deliberate misrepresentation does. But the metadata is what makes the misrepresentation detectable.</p>

    <p>For professionals handling documents that may enter legal proceedings, the principle is simple: never present a document as what it isn't, and strip metadata before sharing to prevent accidental contradictions from creating impressions you didn't intend.</p>

