    <!-- FAQ -->
    <div class="faq-section">
      <h2 class="faq-title">#11. Frequently asked</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Can PDF metadata contradict what the document says?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Yes, frequently. PDF metadata and visible document content are completely independent. A document can display a date of January 1st while its CreationDate shows March 15th. It can say "Final Version" while its RevisionNumber shows 47 saves. It can claim original authorship while the Author field names a different person or organisation. The metadata is written automatically by software and reflects actual system behaviour, not what the author chose to display.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">What is a PDF metadata mismatch?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">A PDF metadata mismatch occurs when information in the hidden metadata fields, including CreationDate, ModDate, Author, Producer, and RevisionNumber, contradicts what is visible in the document. These mismatches can be accidental (timezone differences, template reuse, auto-save behaviour) or deliberate (backdating, concealing editing history). Either way, they are readable by anyone with free tools like ExifTool.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">How do I check if a PDF's metadata matches its stated date?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Run <code>exiftool document.pdf</code> and look at the Create Date and Modify Date fields. Compare these to the date printed in the document. If the Create Date is later than the document's stated date, the file was created after that date. FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover also shows these fields in its Privacy Risk Score without requiring command-line access.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Can you tell if a PDF was created from a template?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Often yes. If a PDF's CreationDate is significantly older than its ModDate, it was created from an older source. The xmpMM:DocumentID field persists across documents derived from the same original. Two "independent" proposals with the same DocumentID came from the same template. A high RevisionNumber on a supposedly new document also suggests it was built on a heavily-edited existing file.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Are PDF metadata mismatches illegal?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Accidental mismatches are not illegal. Deliberate mismatches created to deceive, including backdating a contract, falsifying authorship in evidence, or altering a signed document, can constitute fraud, evidence tampering, or forgery. In the UK, deliberately presenting a modified document as the original may violate the Fraud Act 2006. In the US, falsifying records in federal proceedings may violate 18 U.S.C. § 1519.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">How do I remove metadata to prevent contradictions?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
